{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a sustained rise, the model’s level is biased low and the gap widens with horizon (small error at days 1–3, large error by days 10–30). Apply an increasing positive uplift with forecast horizon.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts upward (it projects sideways/mean-reverting paths while realized prices keep trending). If recent returns are persistently positive, reduce mean-reversion strength and increase trend-following component.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors are not constant—underprediction grows roughly monotonically with lead time. Use a lead-time scaling correction (e.g., +k * horizon) rather than a flat add-on.",
    "Post-breakout ceiling effect: after a breakout to new highs, the model caps prices in the high-60s/low-70s while outcomes move into mid-70s to 80s. If price exceeds recent 30d max, shift forecast distribution upward (raise implied ceiling).",
    "Underestimation of volatility/step changes: realized paths show large step-ups; forecasts are too smooth. In high-momentum regimes, widen forecast band and allow discrete jumps (higher day-to-day drift/vol)."
  ]
}
